# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out native.
#
# This is not tidiness. The repository is authored on Windows and built on Linux CI, and two
# files break outright if they arrive with CRLF: `gradlew` and any shell script, because the
# kernel reads `#!/bin/sh\r` as an interpreter that does not exist. Relying on each
# contributor's `core.autocrlf` leaves that to a setting nobody checks.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by a shell, so the line ending is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
*.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf

# Byte-for-byte, never transformed.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary

# Excluded from `git archive`, which is how a release tarball is built.
#
# Mostly belt and braces: `git archive` exports tracked files only, so anything `.gitignore`
# already covers cannot appear. The entries stay because the failure they guard against is
# expensive and silent - a `.env` holding live credentials sits beside the source, is invisible
# to `git status`, and travels in any archive built by zipping the working directory. Rotating
# a leaked key is the only remedy afterwards.
#
# The real protection is `scripts/package_release.py`, which builds the tarball and then greps
# it for secret-shaped strings. A rule missing here fails that check instead of shipping.
.env export-ignore
.env.* export-ignore
*.iml export-ignore
